    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `summarize_ticket` in `app/summary.py`:\n\n```python\ndef summarize_ticket(chat: BaseChatModel, ticket: Ticket) -> str:\n    \"\"\"A one-line summary of `ticket`; generation stops at the first blank line.\"\"\"\n    messages = [SystemMessage(SUMMARY_PROMPT), HumanMessage(ticket_text(ticket))]\n    reply = chat.invoke(messages, stop=[\"\\n\\n\"])\n    # `.text` joins the text blocks, so this also works for models that reply with a list of content blocks.\n    return str(reply.text).strip()\n```\n\nIt makes one `invoke` call with a system message and a user message, and passes `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]`. I used `reply.text` rather than `str(reply.content)` because some chat models, such as Anthropic's, return the reply as a list of content blocks. Calling `str()` on that list would give you the list's text form instead of the reply.\n\n**Testing:** `pytest -q` passes (2 tests), but neither test covers this function. I checked it separately with a fake chat model that replies in content blocks. The model received exactly one request: a `SystemMessage` and a `HumanMessage`, with stop `['\\n\\n']`. The function returned `'Acme site down'` as a plain `str`.\n\n**Two things to know:**\n- **Leftover file:** the check script `check_summary_tmp.py` is still in the project root because deleting it needed your approval. You can delete it.\n- **Same bug in `app/translate.py`:** it still uses `str(message.content)`, so it will break the same way with models that reply in content blocks. I didn't change it because it was outside the task."
